\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Colombia's Jan-Aug Trade Surplus $1.029 Bln -DANE  \b0\par\par\uc2 324 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.    \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Nov 03, 2005 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 Colombia posted a trade surplus of $1.029 billion during the first eight months of the year, widening from a surplus of $621.9 million in the same year-earlier period, according to official data published late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The larger surplus is attributed to a surge in exports, which were up 30% in the period to $13.72 billion, government statistics department DANE said.  \par \par \uc2 DANE said coal exports rose 55% between January and August to $1.706 billion as the country benefitted from rising commodity prices. Oil exports rose 32% between January and August to $3.46 billion.  \par \par \uc2 In August alone, Colombian exports totaled $1.87 billion, up 24% from August 2004, due to a surge of traditional exports, especially higher revenues from coal and oil exports and to non-traditional exports. Non-traditional exports rose 6% to $929.4 million.  \par \par \uc2 Colombia posted its largest bilateral surplus in the first eight months of the year - $1.683 billion - with the U.S. The strong surplus with the U.S. is partly due to the Andean Trade Preferences and Drug Eradication Act, or ATPDEA, an accord that gives tariff-free access on goods to the U.S. market in exchange for cooperation in clamping down on illegal drugs. Colombian exports of leather goods, textiles and clothing, among others, have skyrocketed, due to the ATPDEA.  \par \par \uc2 The second-largest trade surplus was with neighboring Venezuela, where an economic recovery has been taking place.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants were particularly pleased with the export figure, which rose despite the strengthening of the peso. A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would normally erode the competitiveness of non-traditional exports and spur imports. The peso has appreciated 4.7% so far this year, despite constant \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 -By Diana Delgado, Dow Jones Newswires; 573153088934; \b \highlight22\uc2 colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 @dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 11-03-05 1750ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020051103e1b3008ya\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
