\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Fin Min: To Continue  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkts  \b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2005\par 22:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Alberto Carrasquilla said Sunday that Colombian authorities will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the peso's rise against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as inflation keeps coming down, as long as interest rates don't give us the sense that there is turmoil in the future...as long as we keep meeting fiscal targets, we will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said in an interview with reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if he saw any fiscal or monetary clouds on the horizon that would make the Colombian authorities stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Carrasquilla said: "Not at all...I don't see interest rates going up, I don't see inflationary expectations going up, I don't see models showing us that inflation should be a concern."  \par \par \uc2 Colombia's peso gained to its strongest level since August 2002 on Friday to COP2,288.75 against the U.S. dollar. It has appreciated nearly 4% against the dollar this year on heavy portfolio inflows, foreign direct investment, strong export revenue and remittances from Colombians living abroad.  \par \par \uc2 The peso has gained despite \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth $2.8 billion so far from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In August alone, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $999.6 million in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in what was considered its strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ever in a single month. [ 09-25-05 1731ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Carrasquilla said that the Colombian government will continue to work toward better debt liability management, and is interested in repurchasing more debt that it issued overseas if market conditions remain favorable.  \par \par \uc2 Responding to a question on whether he plans more debt buybacks, such as the transaction Colombia conducted earlier this month, Carrasquilla said: "Yes I do."  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in September, the Colombian government bought back around $1.1 billion in overseas debt and issued $500 million in longer-dated paper to improve its debt profile and remove less liquid bonds from the market, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We reduced the external exposure of our debt by $600 million," he said. "We are always looking for the opportunity to improve the characteristics of our debt, in size, composition, duration, rollover risk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk - all of the dimensions."  \par \par \uc2 He said the Colombian government doesn't need to go to the market to fund its budget needs for 2006 as it has already raised the money. "But that doesn't mean we won't do trades. As long as conditions are good, we will do it in terms of enhancing our curve, or even going for more prefinancing."  \par \par \uc2 He said that tapping the international capital markets for its 2007 financing needs this year is a "possibility."  \par \par \uc2 There is currently a lot of enthusiasm among investors for emerging market paper and the risk premium on bonds from those issuers is currently trading at historic lows.  \par \par \uc2 Carrasquilla also said the government may issue more peso-denominated bonds in the overseas market, just as it did at the end of 2004. Earlier this month, Brazil also issued its first-ever real denominated bond in the external debt market in a placement that was highly successful.  \par \par \uc2 "We are certainly interested in issuing more" peso-denominated bonds overseas, he said. But he noted that the existing peso-denominated overseas bond has some liquidity problems. Carrasquilla said that he can't say whether issuing peso-denominated debt is better than dollar-denominated bonds.  \par \par \uc2 The finance minister said his economy continues to sustain a growth rate in the 4% area. He said that foreign direct investment in Colombia is likely to exceed earlier expectations of $3 billion this year. Carrasquilla forecasts FDI this year at $3.5 billion, a 40% jump from 2004.  \par \par \uc2 -By Shumita Sharma, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-216-9270; shumita.sharma@dowjones.com [ 09-25-05 1811ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020050925e19p0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
