\par\par \b \uc2 Colombia peso closes strongly as inflows rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 389 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2005\par 23:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Colombia, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The Colombian peso closed under the psychologically important level of 2,300 to the dollar on Tuesday, the strongest close in three years, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported robust foreign capital inflows bolstering the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The peso <COP=RR> closed at 2,294 to the greenback, the strongest since May 2002, defying efforts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect Colombian exporters from a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  already up 11.55 percent against the dollar over the last 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 Last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and bought between $500 million and $600 million, its biggest ever one-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Foreign capital inflows to \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew by 16.7 percent in the first seven months of this year compared with the same 2004 period, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 But the growth rate of inflows in the first seven months of 2005 marked a slowdown from the 19.6 percent recorded in the first six months of the year, the bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The stronger the peso, the more expensive Colombia's exports are overseas.  \par \par \uc2 Many Colombian exporters are expected to earn record revenue this year thanks to high global prices for coal, oil and coffee. Other exporters, such as those in the Andean country's huge flower business, are not benefiting from high international prices and say the strong peso threatens to put them out of business.  \par \par \uc2 In the first seven months of 2005, foreign capital inflows totaled $7.896 billion, compared with $6.785 billion from January though July 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Inflows during the first seven months of the year from exports of commodities and industrial goods totaled $2.582 billion, an expansion of 29.7 percent. The gain marked a slowdown from 36.2 percent in the first half.  \par \par \uc2 Remittances from Colombians working overseas totaled $1.969 billion in the January though July period, up 8.66 percent from the 2004 period, but under the 10-percent growth in the first half of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign direct investment reached $1.875 billion in the first seven months of 2005, up 59.6 percent from the 2004 period but under the 79.2 percent expansion in the first half of this year.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-COLOMBIA-INFLOWS|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050816e18g0022o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
