\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk May Take Steps To Offset  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 196 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2005\par 23:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said Wednesday the bank will keep purchasing dollars to contain the peso's strength, but may take steps to limit the impact on the broader economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The bank is considering the possibility of selling Treasury notes to sterilize dollar purchases," Jose Dario Uribe said at a seminar.  \par \par \uc2 Via a strategy known as sterilization, central banks use open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counteract the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the money supply and the broader economy. In the past, the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has used such strategies, particularly towards the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought a net $1.615 billion in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market between January and June in an effort to stem the peso's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The Colombian peso strengthened to its highest level in more than three years Tuesday, at COP2,305.95. The peso has gained 3.6% so far this year, after rising more than 18% in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 -By Diana Delgado; Dow Jones Newswires; 571-600-1980; colombia@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Luis Enrique Hurtado contributed to this article.) [ 03-08-05 2150GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050803e183000zv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
