\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Colombia needs fiscal reforms for surplus-minister.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy   \par \uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2004\par 23:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Colombia's economy is recovering but tax and pension reforms are needed to improve the government's fiscal situation, Finance Minister Alberto Carrasquilla told Reuters on Thursday.    \par \par \uc2  "The most important issue for us is to reduce the ratio of debt to GDP, and among the six reform bills in Congress, the pension system reform is the most important," Carrasquilla said in an interview.    \par \par \uc2  Currently, the government uses 3.14 percent of its income to pay pensions, but pension expenditures are up 5 percent of its gross domestic product (GDP).    \par \par \uc2  If the constitutional amendment is passed, then pension expenditures would likely go down to 3.8 percent of GDP, Carrasquilla said.    \par \par \uc2  In addition, if the tax reform is passed, the government would earn an extra 0.66 percent of GDP and be able to cover the pension expenditures to 3.8 percent.    \par \par \uc2  "But the pension problem is one of liquidity," he said.    \par \par \uc2  Colombia's public debt should fall to 52 percent of gross domestic product this year, the International Monetary Fund said early this month.    \par \par \uc2  PENSIONS AND TAXES    \par \par \uc2  Colombia might struggle to persuade international markets to buy 30 year bonds right now, though a temporary tax surcharge to service the debt might be an inducement.    \par \par \uc2  The second possibility would be to charge temporary taxes that would be given back later.    \par \par \uc2  But the country's best bet to solve its liquidity problem, after passing the pension and tax bills, would be to issue a 20-year bond to Colombia's private pension funds, which are flooded with money as many contributors are young.    \par \par \uc2  "We would issue bonds to the funds as they don't need the amount of liquidity that they have right now, and in 20 years, when they start needing that cash, we will give it back," Carrasquilla said. "But they will not negotiate with us until we have passed those amendments in Congress."    \par \par \uc2  The tax reform, which proposes expanding Colombia's value-added tax, or VAT, to basics like milk and medical services, faces tough opposition in Congress.    \par \par \uc2  Santiago Castro, chairman of the Lower House Economic Affairs Committee, said on Wednesday he would have to water down the proposal to ensure passage.    \par \par \uc2  Supporters say the bill is key to ensuring Colombia's long-term solvency, while the government tries to end a costly four-decade-old guerrilla war that kills thousands every year.    \par \par \uc2  But with 60 percent of Colombians living in poverty and many struggling to eat, it will be difficult to convince lawmakers who want to be reelected in 2006.    \par \par \uc2  Carrasquilla said the tax taken from the poor would be given back - possibly in the form of food coupons - but the tax taken from the well-off would be used half for social policies for the poor and the other half for fiscal adjustments.    \par \par \uc2  "Public expenditure has always been the way to do social justice. If there is a Value Aggregate Tax there has to be social policies in place," the minister said.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ENERGY AND IMF    \par \par \uc2  However, Colombia's economy is growing by 4 percent or more a year, supported by market confidence.    \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2   has been rising, pushed up by capital inflows, a rise in stocks, real estate and private investment, Carrasquilla said.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by around $2 billion or 2 percent of GDP, and by the end of the year the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have bought US$3 billion.    \par \par \uc2  In terms of exploration and production in the oil sector, Carrasquilla said contractual conditions and security issues have substantially improved.    \par \par \uc2  "In order to recuperate production levels of 700,000 barrels a day we need to explore 60 wells per year taking into account probability of success and our geography," he said.    \par \par \uc2  In 1999 only 6 wells were explored due to lack of funds and unappealing contracts.    \par \par \uc2  "The 'State Take', or the portion that the country takes from the venture, was worse than in Cuba," the minister said. "There was no incentive for private companies."    \par \par \uc2  This year Colombia is to have explored 35 new wells.    \par \par \uc2  Also, with a strong economic recovery, Colombia is looking to graduate from the International Monetary Fund loan program soon. "We want to graduate. We are not in a crisis anymore. We don't need that backing, and we can do it taking half a step," he said.    \par \par \uc2  The current agreement is a stand-by of two years with 100 percent of the quota, but Colombia would want an agreement of one year or less-than two years, with 50 percent of the quota.    \par \par \uc2  The current quota is $2.2 billion. (Reporting by Manuela Badawy, editing by Toni Reinhold; Reuters Messaging: manuela.badawy.reuters.com@reuters.net; Tel +1 646 223 6055)).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040930e09u00kzp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
