\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Colombia Cenbank cuts rates by 25 basis points.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier Mozzo  \par \uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 2004\par 21:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOGOTA, Colombia, Feb 20 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut all of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by 25 basis points on Friday, aiming to add fuel to the country's economic recovery with the first easing of monetary policy in well over a year.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts surveyed by Reuters had widely expected monetary policy to be left unchanged, saying they thought a February rate cut would be premature given uncertainty on the year's inflation outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Colombia has agreed to meet a 2004 inflation target of 5-6 percent, plus or minus two percentage points, under a $2.1 billion loan accord with the International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \uc2 But acting bank manager, Jose Dario Uribe, said the low-inflationary environment - despite a brightening outlook for economic growth - made it an opportune time to cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 "The decision was taken after analyzing the inflation forecasts in the medium-and long-term, the low trend of basic inflation and inflation forecasts, and the situation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he said, reading a statement.  \par \par \uc2 The bank cut its minimum repurchase agreement to 7.00 percent, its Lombard repo rate to 10.75 percent, its minimum reverse repo rate to 6.00 percent, and its Lombard reverse repo rate to 5.00 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The move follows a recent appreciation of the peso , which has gained more than 8 percent against the U.S. dollar in 12 months to Feb. 20.  \par \par \uc2 The government, worried the appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hurt exports and sap growth, is actively trying to tighten supply of greenbacks to restrain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . President Alvaro Uribe has been publicly calling for lower interest rates since last year.  \par \par \uc2 The Colombian economy expanded by 3.5 percent in 2003, its fastest rate in six years, according to preliminary government figures. The government forecasts growth of 3.8 percent in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Alberto Carrasquilla told reporters that he hoped the rate cut would translate into cheaper consumer credit, and stimulate demand for pesos.  \par \par \uc2 The last adjustment to rates was on April 28, 2003, when they were raised across-the-board by 1 percentage point.  \par \par \uc2 Colombia \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not cut rates since 2002. That year, monetary policy makers slashed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by an across-the-board 325 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 The Andean nation met its 2003 IMF inflation target. But its 6.49 percent rise in consumer prices slightly breached a voluntary \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  target of 6.0 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040220e02k002by\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
