\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-Colombia's Uribe asks cenbank to halt peso rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2003\par 21:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, Colombia, May 14 (Reuters) - Colombian President Alvaro Uribe Wednesday called for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the strengthening of the peso, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped against the U.S. dollar after his statement. \par \par \uc2  "We trust that the Bank of the Republic (\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will take all the measures to comply with what we said in January - keep the exchange rate in the levels it was at in January, keep it stable in real terms and at a competitive level for economic recovery," Uribe told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Uribe's call was the latest volley in a so-far unsuccessful campaign to pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate interest rates. On April 28 the bank raised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by one percentage point to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets inflation targets, snubbing earlier government calls to hold rates unchanged to protect growth. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board is next due to meet on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect it will leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Colombian 2012 TES bonds are yielding 14.14 percent, a return attractive enough to lure investors into pesos. \par \par \uc2  The peso slipped sharply following the president's remarks, falling 1.66 percent to close at 2,843 per U.S. dollar. On Tuesday, it had closed up 1.08 percent at 2,796 - its highest level against the greenback since Dec. 5. \par \par \uc2  At Wednesday's opening price, the peso was up 3.8 percent against the dollar over the past month. It started January at 2,865 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The peso lost 25 percent of its value against the dollar in 2002, far outpacing depreciation forecasts from both the government and the private sector. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  frets that the peso fall increased inflationary expectations and pushed up debt servicing costs for both government and companies. \par \par \uc2  The bank's interest rate rise last month was a bid to keep 2003 inflation at the maximum 5.5 percent agreed under the terms of a $2.1 billion loan accord with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  But the government wants a competitive peso to ensure the health of Colombia's export sector, which has been hit by a sharp economic contraction and draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls in former number two market Venezuela. \par \par \uc2  Solid economic growth and cutting a 17 percent unemployment rate are vital to Uribe's hopes of eradicating the poverty which feeds Colombia's four-decade-old war and its massive cocaine industry. He also needs a robust economy to keep fiscal accounts in order as he boosts military spending by a third. \par \par \uc2  The government expects Latin America's fifth-largest economy to expand by 2 percent this year, after anemic 1.5 percent growth in 2002. \par \par \uc2  "During this government, we are not going to go back to the revaluations that did so much damage to the country, which affected employment and production so much," Uribe said. \par \par \uc2  The bank had no plans to respond to Uribe, a spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts doubted the president's call would have much influence on the seven-member board, over which Finance Minister Roberto Junguito presides. \par \par \uc2  "Its decisions are always based on the long-term and we think this rise in the peso is transitory," said one Bogota analyst, asking not to be named. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030514dz5e0112u\par