\par\par \b \uc2  SURVEY: Depreciation Fueled Colombian Inflation In July \b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2002\par 19:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Consumer Price Index:\par           Jul     Jun     May     Apr    Jul/01  12-Mo/01\par Forecast +0.19%  +0.14%  +0.45%  +0.64%  +0.06%   +7.63%\par Actual    ---    +0.43%  +0.60%  +0.92%  +0.11%   +7.65%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Mary Morrison\par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BOGOTA -(Dow Jones)- Costlier imports, amid local-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par depreciation, apparently fueled Colombian inflation in July, although\par this was offset by cheaper foods.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Dow Jones Newswires survey of seven analysts Friday produced an\par average estimate of a 0.19% increase in the consumer price index for the\par month. The projections ranged from +0.02% to +0.28%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par National statistics department DANE is due to report July CPI data\par Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "On the one hand, food prices appear to have fallen last month," said\par Juan Manuel Velasco, head of research at Medellin investment firm\par Inversionistas de Colombia. "But there are fears that the falling peso\par is driving up inflation."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Colombia's peso ($1=COP2639.05) has fallen 12% against the dollar\par since the start of June, and is down 14% for 2002 to date.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, Velasco added that the effect on inflation will be muted by\par the relatively low weighting imports have in Colombia's CPI.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Most food - the heaviest weighted group, with 30% of the total - is\par produced locally. And while international oil prices influence gasoline\par costs, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Colombia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to smooth these locally.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Overall, analysts say lackluster macroeconomic conditions continue to\par dampen aggregate demand, which weakens inflationary pressure.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government projects 2002 economic growth to fall to 1.5%, from\par last year's 1.6%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Colombia's urban jobless rate stood at 18.0% in late June, among the\par highest levels in Latin America.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Looking forward, the survey projected annual inflation to fall to\par 6.05% for 2002 as a whole, in line with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target of\par 6.00%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Consumer prices rose 7.65% last year, which was inside the bank's 8.0%\par goal and the slightest yearly increase since 1970.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Mary Morrison, Dow Jones Newswires; 571-602-1402;\par colombia@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020802dy820011g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
