\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market to buoy peso. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier Mozzo \par \uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2002\par 01:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 1 (Reuters) - Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it sold $180 million in dollar-purchase options to support the country's sinking peso , but the bank's second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a week failed to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  Nervous markets have slashed nearly 9 percent off the peso's value in the past week, partly over worries about Colombia's swelling public debt and debt load. \par \par \uc2  But the pummeling is also due to fallout from regional financial crisis spreading northward from Brazil and Argentina. Risk-weary investors are selling off the peso, and banks are buying dollars after seeing their stocks of greenbacks cut in half since December. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said $69 million, or about 38 percent, of the dollar-purchase options sold on Thursday were exercised later in the day. The options were valid through Aug. 30 and carried a rate of 2,636 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The peso, meanwhile, lost a slight 0.05 percent of its value on Thursday, closing at 2,639 per dollar. It opened stronger against Wednesday at 2,627 pesos per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The peso has lost about 13 percent of its value so far this year. Its accentuated slide against the greenback compares with a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast of an 8 percent depreciation for all of 2002 and a loss of just 2.7 percent against the dollar last year. \par \par \uc2  Until this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since floating the peso in 1999. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday relaxed guidelines on future \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to give itself more leeway to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bank said that, after Thursday, it was scrapping the $180 million limit on future \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  option auctions as well as a rule stopping it from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again until all options are exercised. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that the country's banking system had seen its stock of dollars fall by 50 percent since December to $200 million. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  General Manager Miguel Urrutia said on Friday there was no liquidity crisis or need for the government to raise foreign reserves, which early this week hovering near a record high with $10.81 billion. \par \par \uc2  The bank is forecasting that foreign reserves will total $10.48 billion at the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  The war-torn Andean nation, fighting Marxist rebels for 38 years, ended 2001 with record high foreign reserves of $10.16 billion, surpassing an IMF-agreed minimum of $9.14 billion for the year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020801dy8100uv7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
