\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Tweaks  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Rules \b0\par\par\uc2 290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 August 2002\par 15:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOGOTA -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board late Wednesday altered the conditions under which it must \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the local peso (COP). \par \par \uc2  The basic rule is unchanged. The bank must auction call options, through which it can sell $180 million to financial  institutions, whenever the benchmark Representative Market Rate, or TRM, is 4.0% above or below its 20-day moving average. The TRM is a weighted average exchange rate of the previous session's trades. \par \par \uc2  But the bank said in a statement that it has eliminated the additional requirement that all outstanding options be used. \par \par \uc2  The institution devised the antivolatility rules in 1999, when it scrapped the peso's fixed trading band against  the dollar and allowed the peso to float freely. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not triggered until Monday, when the bank held its first auction of call options. \par \par \uc2  The bank's second sale - covering $180 million - is Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The day's TRM is COP2636.30 to the dollar, which is above the COP2628.16 trigger level. The bank said all Monday's options have been exercised. \par \par \uc2  The peso strengthened slightly Wednesday, as financial institutions closed positions for the end of the month. \par \par \uc2  The final exchange rate fell for the first time in eight  sessions, to COP2636.70 to the dollar from Tuesday's record-weak COP2645.00. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still down 12% against the dollar since the start of June, and has fallen 14% for 2002 to date. \par \par \uc2  Traders blame a shortage of dollar liquidity among local  financial institutions, jitters about Brazil's falling real  and anticipatory demand for dollars from nervous international companies. \par \par \uc2  -By Mary Morrison, Dow Jones Newswires; 571-602-1402; colombia@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020801dy81000xn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
