\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Colombian peso slides despite Cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nelson Bocanegra \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2002\par 21:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's embattled peso closed sharply lower on Tuesday as concern over the country's growing debt load and Latin America's simmering financial crisis outweighed the effects of Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed down 1.63 percent at a new historic low of 2,645 per U.S. dollar, in the seventh straight session of depreciation against the greenback. Trade, at $475 million, was healthy as financial institutions and companies bought U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet end-of-month financial obligations. \par \par \uc2  The peso had earlier touched 2,654 per dollar before pulling back. Local banks have been squeezed by reductions in their foreign credit lines over the past month over fears of regional economic contagion. \par \par \uc2  Colombian debt also suffered, both abroad and at home. The spread on Colombia's global bonds has now broadened to 801 points over U.S. Treasuries, up from 618 points on May 30. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $180 million in dollar options to local financial institutions on Monday in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the peso was floated in 1999. \par \par \uc2  The move, triggered automatically by a 4 percent decline over a 20-day period, was designed to halt a decline which has lopped more than 13 percent off the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value so far this year and has far outstripped a forecast by \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials of an 8 percent depreciation over all of 2002. \par \par \uc2  "The speculation continues. They (the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) haven't put out the fire, there is still demand for dollars," said Eduardo Reyes of brokerage Afin, citing regional woes as contributors to the peso's decline. \par \par \uc2  The peso fell a slight 2.7 percent against the dollar in 2001. \par \par \uc2  BRAZILIAN TREMOR SHAKES COLOMBIA \par \par \uc2  On top of Argentina's seemingly endless crisis, the market has been unnerved by signs that South American giant Brazil is staggering into election-year trouble. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real sank to a new record low of 3.305 per U.S. dollar before recouping to 3.260 at midday, down 7.5 centavos from Monday's close. It is almost 30 percent weaker than where it began the year. \par \par \uc2  In another Latin American financial trouble zone, Uruguay's peso plunged by nearly a quarter of its value after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declared a bank holiday. \par \par \uc2  Markets are also increasingly worried over Colombia's rising debt levels, with its debt-to-GDP ratio at nearly 60 percent as of March, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data. \par \par \uc2  On Colombia's local debt market, domestic TES 2012 bonds spiked to 15.78 percent on the secondary market, after opening at 15 percent. \par \par \uc2  "This is very ugly. It's all because of the regional crisis. We're losing a lot of ground and lots of people are buying dollars and bailing out of TES because no one wants Colombian debt," a broker said. \par \par \uc2  Bogota \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were not overly concerned about the details of the 2003 budget bill, which was sent to Congress on Monday and must be approved this year. The proposed budget would push the fiscal deficit to 3.0 percent from an expected 2.6 percent this year, as the new government of President-elect Alvaro Uribe boosts defense and social spending to tackle a 38-year-old guerrilla war. \par \par \uc2  Uribe's designated finance minister, Roberto Junguito, told reporters in Washington he plans spending cuts in other areas and tax increases to help pay for the government's plans. Junguito, who plans to work closely with the International Monetary Fund, promised "fiscal stability in the mid-term". \par \par \uc2  He saw economic growth at a sluggish 1.0 percent in 2002 - below current government forecasts of 1.5 percent - but picking up to 2.5 percent next year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020730dy7u00yqe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
