\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to support weak peso. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nelson Bocanegra \par \uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2002\par 00:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $180 million in dollar options to buoy its sinking peso on Monday, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still slid to an all-time low even after most of the options were exercised. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the first in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in 1999, was triggered automatically after the peso slipped more than 4 percent over a 20-day period by Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed the slide to fallout from economic woes in other South American nations, including Argentina's debt default and preelection jitters in Brazil that have slashed 20 percent off its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value since January. \par \par \uc2  But after the market close, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed that the country's banking system had seen its stock of dollars fall by 50 percent since December to $200 million - signaling the possibility that Colombian banks may need to buy more dollars to cover foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, further weakening the peso. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that $117 million of the dollar-purchase options sold on Monday had already been exercised, even though buyers had the right to buy dollars at 2,596.26 pesos each until Aug. 28. \par \par \uc2  The peso, which has shed nearly 13 percent of its value against the dollar this year, opened trading on Monday at 2,588.5 per dollar. But after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso quickly reverted to Friday's closing level of 2,600 per dollar and continued its slide during the day to end at a new historic low of 2,601.7 pesos per greenback. \par \par \uc2  Last week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  General Manager Miguel Urrutia estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would depreciate just 8 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  Markets are increasingly worried over Colombia's rising debt levels, with its debt-to-GDP ratio at nearly 50 percent. And traders said that risk-weary foreign banks have reduced the availability of loans to Colombian banks and companies - creating a scarcity of dollars that has weakened the peso. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  General Manager Miguel Urrutia said on Friday there was no liquidity crisis or need for the government to raise foreign reserves, which now hovering near a record high with $10.81 billion, the bank said on Monday. The peso fell a slight 2.7 percent against the dollar in 2001. \par \par \uc2  The bank is forecasting that foreign reserves will total $10.48 billion at the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  The war-torn Andean nation, fighting Marxist rebels for 38 years, ended 2001 with record high foreign reserves of $10.16 billion, surpassing an IMF-agreed minimum of $9.14 billion for the year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020729dy7t00x6u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
