\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  to make peso  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  Monday. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Monica Garcia \par \uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2002\par 02:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell $180 million in dollar options on Monday to support the peso after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breached a 4-percent limit of depreciation over 20 days, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be the first to buoy the peso - which has lost 12 percent against the U.S. dollar this year - since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was floated in September 1999. \par \par \uc2  "On Monday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will sell $180 million in dollar options," Miguel Urrutia, the bank's manager told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday, the peso hit a new historic low, closing at 2,600 pesos per dollar, down 0.33 from Thursday. The Banking Superintendency later calculated the average exchange rate for Monday at 2,596 per dollar - enough to trigger an automatic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rules, the institution is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the peso's average exchange rate in the last 20 day varies by more than 4 percent against the daily average exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Urrutia said there was "no need" to raise foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - now at a record high - and added there was no dollar liquidity problem in the domestic market. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's foreign reserves stood at $10.86 billion in the week ended July 17 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forecasting that foreign reserves will total $10.48 billion at the end of 2002. \par \par \uc2  Speaking to reporters after attending a government ceremony at the Presidential Palace, Urrutia said the exchange rate is "consistent" with a government forecast of current account deficit of 2 percent of gross domestic product for 2002. \par \par \uc2  Colombia posted a current account deficit of $384 million for the first quarter of 2002 - equivalent to about 1.8 percent of gross domestic product. In all of 2001, Colombia ran up a current account shortfall of $1.69 billion, equivalent to about 2 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  Although the country has no official target for the current account deficit with the International Monetary Fund, the IMF does monitor the figure as part of a $2.7-billion loan accord with the Andean nation. \par \par \uc2  PHLEGMATIC ABOUT DEPRECIATION \par \par \uc2  The government has been remarkably phlegmatic about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation - fueled by Colombia's foreign debt load and regional jitters spreading from Argentina and Brazil - saying it is not worried and that it should help exporters. \par \par \uc2  Local analysts have been taken by surprise by this year's fall and many are unsure about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behavior over the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Latin America's fifth-largest economy - which benefits from large but hard to quantify illegal cash flows from cocaine sales - defied forecasts in 2001, when it hardly budged against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts say that the peso could bounce back in 2002, buoyed by the lack of serious weaknesses in an economy that may be sluggish - the government is forecasting a growth of 1.5 percent in 2002 - but has not set off alarm bells. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's debt load - at about 60 percent of gross domestic product - has some economists concerned, especially since President-elect Alvaro Uribe plans big increases in military and social spending in a country gripped by a 38-year-old guerrilla war. Uribe takes office Aug. 7. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020727dy7r000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
