rding to local analysts, an average exchange rate of 2,596 pesos per greenback would prompt an auction of dollar-sale options for Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not pronounced itself. \par \par \uc2  PHLEGMATIC GOVERNMENT \par \par \uc2  Although the peso came within a whisker of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks ago, the government has been remarkably phlegmatic about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation, saying it is not worried and that it should help exporters. \par \par \uc2  Local analysts have been taken by surprise by this year's fall and many are unsure about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behavior over the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Latin America's fifth-largest economy - which benefits from large but hard to quantify illegal cash flows from cocaine sales - defied forecasts in 2001, when it hardly budged against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts say that the peso could bounce back in 2002, buoyed by the lack of serious weaknesses in an economy that may be sluggish - the government is forecasting a growth of 1.5 percent in 2002 - but has not set off alarm bells. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's debt load - at about 60 percent of gross domestic product - has some economists concerned, especially since President-elect Alvaro Uribe plans big increases in military and social spending in a country gripped by a 38-year-old guerrilla war. Uribe takes office Aug. 7. \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Rengifo also announced the government will auction in August $50 million in put options to bolster its foreign reserves. Colombia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are at a record high - they rose rose $43.6 million, or 0.4 percent, to $10.86 billion in the week ended July 17. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is forecasting 