\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  peso weakens, but no Cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2002\par 01:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso fell tantalizingly close to a four-percent limit for depreciation over 20 days on Thursday but the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that it had not reached a key level which would trigger automatic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  The peso was calculated at 2,506.84 per U.S. dollar on Thursday, just above the 2,507.66 limit which would have meant it had lost 4.0 percent over 20 days, the Banking Superintendency said. \par \par \uc2  According to data earlier in the afternoon from Colombia's electronic trading system, the peso had lost 0.38 percent to close at 2,509 per dollar on Thursday. This level would have been easily enough to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had said it was prepared to sell $180 million in options to sell dollars on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's market data showed the peso plumbing a new historic minimum for the 12th consecutive session in a slump fueled by Colombia's foreign debt load and regional jitters spreading northwards from Argentina and Brazil. \par \par \uc2  Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have been the first to buoy up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which has lost 9.88 percent against the U.S. dollar this year - since before it was floated in September 1999. \par \par \uc2  Under \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules, the institution is obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if the peso varies by at least 4 percent over 20 days. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Juan Manuel Santos earlier said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's depreciation was not worrying and should help exporters. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the devaluation year-on-year, or for the year so far, it is a very modest depreciation and there is no reason for us to worry at all," Santos told reporters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Latin America's fifth-largest economy - which benefits from large but hard to quantify illegal cash flows from cocaine sales - defied forecasts in 2001, when it hardly budged against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The government's official estimate was for a depreciation of only 8.0 percent for all of this year. Local analysts have been taken by surprise by this year's fall and many are unsure about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behavior over the rest of the year. \par \par \uc2  Some say that the peso could bounce back, buoyed by the lack of serious weaknesses in an economy that may be sluggish but has not set off alarm bells. \par \par \uc2  SANTOS SAYS A WEAKER PESO IS GOOD \par \par \uc2  "A devaluation is welcome, because it helps exporters regain lost competitiveness," Santos said. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's exports were down in the first four months of this year, at $3.87 billion compared to the $4.03 billion recorded from January to April of last year, although this was still enough to record a $207 million surplus. \par \par \uc2  Most of the country's exports come from areas like oil, coal and coffee, whose prices depend on international markets, but sales of products such as flowers, textiles and leather have grown strongly this year. \par \par \uc2  Some economists are concerned Colombia's debt load is getting too heavy - especially as President-elect Alvaro Uribe plans big increases in military and social spending in a country gripped by a 38-year-old guerrilla war. Gross public debt is at about 60 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  But Colombia's foreign reserves are at a record high, at about $10.8 billion - well above targets agreed to under the three-year $2.7-billion loan accord with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020711dy7b00uv5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
