\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Colombia cuts rates, pegs 2001 GDP growth 1.5 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier Mozzo \par \uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2002\par 18:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, Colombia, Jan 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates on Friday, most by 50 basis points, in an effort to speed economic growth the finance ministry said exceeded 1.5 percent last year. \par \par \uc2  "We took this decision to fuel greater economic growth, and because we are doing well on the issue of inflation," said Finance Minister Juan Manuel Santos, who sits on the bank's weekly rate-setting meetings. \par \par \uc2  Under the terms of a three-year, $2.7 billion IMF loan agreement which expires at the end of this year, Colombia must meet an inflation target of 6 percent or less in 2002. It met all IMF-agreed targets last year, including key ceilings on inflation and the fiscal deficit. \par \par \uc2  "All of the models (for inflation) show that we will meet this 6 percent inflation target this year," Santos told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts branded the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision on rates "aggressive." Friday's rate cut marked the first relaxation in monetary policy this year, and followed a 350 basis point reduction in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in 2001. \par \par \uc2  "It is aggressive. I don't know if the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took this decision to nudge the economy and business, or if it is to (weaken the peso)," said Alexander Cardenas, an analyst at Bogota-based Acciones y Valores. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's peso has climbed in value against the dollar by 1.14 percent since the beginning of the year, following a much lower-than-expected 2 percent devaluation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Santos said Colombia's economy grew by more than 1.5 percent last year, the same figure published by the government on Dec. 21. \par \par \uc2  With Friday's cut, Colombia's one-day reverse repurchase agreement rate now stands at 7.0 percent, down from 7.50 percent, while repos are at 8.0 percent, against 8.50 percent previously. \par \par \uc2  The "Lombard contraction rate," or the rate for reverse-repo outside-auction bank loans to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fell by 25 basis points to 6.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The "Lombard expansion rate," or the rate for repo, outside-auction \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to the financial system, were cut 50 basis points to 11.75 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020118dy1i00z6p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
