\par\par \b \uc2  Colombian Cenbank more ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 329 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2001\par 22:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, Dec 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday that it was modifying its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market guidelines to allow it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more strongly, even when faced with a smaller range of peso volatility. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that the move was probably aimed at keeping a tight lid on Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which has been relatively stable over the past year - ahead of the presidential election in May. \par \par \uc2  The Bank will now permit itself to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the peso's average value shifts by more than 4.0 percent over a period of 20 working days, compared to a previous limit of 5.0 percent, it said in a news release. \par \par \uc2  The Bank also said it could now spend up to $180 million in a single day's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared to the previous limit of $120 million in a market which usually only sees daily trade of about $170 million. \par \par \uc2  Even though it is giving itself more leeway, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has never needed to utilize its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanisms since it floated the peso in September 1999. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso closed 0.52 percent lower against the U.S. dollar at 2,303 pesos on Wednesday, hit by what local traders said were government dollar purchases. But the peso has performed more strongly than expected in 2001, and has only retreated about 4 percent against the greenback over the year, compared to a fall of 19 percent in 2000. \par \par \uc2  Latin America's fifth-largest economy is wracked by a 37-year-old guerrilla war and will see economic growth of little more than 1.0 percent this year, but its government has had little trouble meeting its financing needs. \par \par \uc2  Next year's election of a successor to President Andres Pastrana could generate uncertainty, particularly regarding the fate of difficult and slow-moving peace talks with leftist rebels - which have been criticized by major candidates. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011226dxcq00zne\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
