\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Colombia's exports seen unchanged in 2001. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nelson Bocanegra \par \uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 23:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CARTAGENA, Colombia, Aug 16 (Reuters) - Hopes of robust growth in Colombian exports for 2001 have been dashed by a strong peso and global economic slowdown, an industry leader said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Instead of a government forecast of 19 percent export growth to $15.50 billion this year, Colombian industry merely hopes to eke out the same level of exports posted in 2000. \par \par \uc2  "We hope to end the year with exports similar to last year, that is $13.1 billion," Javier Diaz, president of the Colombian Association of Exporters (Analdex), told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Hit by a world economic slowdown, exports by Latin America's fifth-largest economy in the first six months of 2001 totaled $6.11 billion, down 3 percent from the $6.3 billion exported in the same period in 2000. In June, exports totaled $990.5 million, down 13.9 percent from the $1.15 billion exported in June of 2000. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's oil production - the country's top foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earner - has suffered badly this year from guerrilla bomb attacks while coffee sales have plummeted due to the collapse in world coffee bean prices. \par \par \uc2  The government cut its growth forecast for 2001 in late July, pegging growth this year at 2.4 percent, down from a previous estimate of 3.8 percent. In 2000, Colombia's economy, boosted by strong exports, grew 2.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  A stronger peso has meant weaker exports, Diaz said. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only weakened 2.79 percent this year, well below inflation of 6.29 percent and a depreciation of 14 percent by the same time last year. \par \par \uc2  The drop of sales in oil and coffee has been offset by a strong performance in exports of nontraditional products including bananas, flowers and leather. Sales from such exports were 15.1 percent higher in the first half at $3.38 billion. \par \par \uc2  "We expect that during the second half of the year, the nontraditional exports will continue showing the same dynamism as they did in the first half so they compensate for the fall in traditional exports," Diaz said. \par \par \uc2  Speaking on the sidelines of a meeting by the National Association of Industrialists (ANDI) in the Caribbean city of Cartagena, Diaz said lower interest rates would jump start economic activity. \par \par \uc2  Coincidentally, \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  slashed its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates on Thursday by a higher-than-expected 100 basis points, citing a June economic slowdown and relative isolation from Argentina's debt default fears. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g01172\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
