\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Colombia holds rates steady on Argentina worries. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Stewart \par \uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2001\par 21:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, Colombia, July 13 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates steady on Friday to guard against fallout from economic troubles in Argentina, Finance Minister Juan Manuel Santos said. \par \par \uc2  "In the meeting (of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directors) we were going to lower rates, but given the situation in Argentina we decided not to lower them," Santos told reporters as he exited the meeting. \par \par \uc2  Fears that Argentina could default on its mountain of debt have rattled markets across the world, battering Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as investors fretted over a potential 1998-style emerging market meltdown. \par \par \uc2  Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not lowered rates since March 12 - when it cut all money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates by 50 basis points. Analysts had said a rate cut would have helped spur growth in Latin America's fifth largest economy. \par \par \uc2  But Santos said that the decision against a rate cut was only a precaution and added that Colombia was well prepared to withstand a ripple-effect from Argentina's economic woes. \par \par \uc2  "Fortunately, the situation (in Argentina) has not affected us, due to the reforms we have implemented and our policy of prefinancing," he said. \par \par \uc2  Colombia has foreign reserves of $9.37 billion and has already secured enough financing to meet its debt payments through the beginning of next year. \par \par \uc2  The government issued a flurry of debt earlier in 2001, including $1 billion of World Bank-backed bonds, a series of smaller euro-denominated issues, and 30 billion yen in four-year "Samurai" bonds in April. \par \par \uc2  ARGENTINA ALSO SLOWS BOND SWAP \par \par \uc2  Colombia has also secured U.S. regulatory approval to issue $2.5 billion of fresh U.S. dollar-denominated debt in a swap for existing U.S. dollar bonds. But Santos said the government would monitor market sentiment, especially with regards to Argentina, before forging ahead with the foreign bond swap. \par \par \uc2  "We don't have any urgency for the swap," Santos told Reuters on Thursday. "We have said many times the swap could be tomorrow, or in a month, or in a year. We will decide this at the moment we see it as convenient." \par \par \uc2  Argentina, Latin America's third-largest economy, is struggling amid a recessionary climate to service its debt - which equals about half of its gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  The country has received debt rating downgrades from three agencies in as many days, with Moody's Investors Service on Friday cutting Argentina's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceiling rating to B3 from B2, and lowering the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank deposit ceiling to Caa1 to B3. It said the risk of default was high and President Fernando de la Rua's proposed spending cuts would only further weaken the economy. \par \par \uc2  Colombia, which has never defaulted on its debt, has considerably higher credit ratings than Argentina. Moody's rates Colombia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  country ceiling at Ba2, while its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bank deposit ceiling ranking is Ba3. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx7d07l5j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
