\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS- \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  peso slides despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Karl Penhaul \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 1999\par 00:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOGOTA, Sept 23 (Reuters) - Traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets wondered aloud on Thursday just how long the country's fragile exchange-rate trading band could survive as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added up the cost of one of its most expensive bids ever to defend the peso. \par \par \uc2  Even as the bank burned $142.4 million in precious foreign reserves during the day, the peso crashed to the floor of its daily exchange band for the third consecutive session to close at 1,993.97 against the dollar. Since Sept. 10, the bank has spent an estimated $290 million from net foreign reserves of some $8.24 billion to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We're holding a minute's silence for the trading band," said Juan Carlos Gonzalez, head of research for Bogota-based brokerage Corredores Asociados. \par \par \uc2  A slew of unfavourable economic news sent industry as well as commercial banks rushing for greenbacks. Just this week, New York-based Standard & Poor's stripped Colombia of its prized investment-grade credit rating and the government released worse-than-expected growth figures, showing the economy contracted more than 6 percent in the first half. \par \par \uc2  Gonzalez did not say when he expected the "funeral" of the so-called crawling-peg regime, introduced in 1994. Under the system, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets daily upper and lower fluctuation limits for the peso in a bid to control the rate of its depreciation. \par \par \uc2  But most Colombian dealers and independent analysts believe it could be just a matter of days before the bank is forced to scrap the system, one of three of its kind surviving in Latin America. \par \par \uc2  They are convinced the International Monetary Fund (IMF) will insist that Colombia agree to end \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls as part of a deal for a potential $3 billion extended fund facility. A final accord is expected within days, IMF chief Michel Camdessus said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One of Gonzalez's colleagues at Corredores Asociados estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only be able to afford \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the current rate for two more days. \par \par \uc2  "Of course, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to say until the last minute that it's not going to scrap the band. It depends how stubborn the bank is. But if it continues selling at this level I think the band can only survive two more days," economic researcher Erika Montanez said. \par \par \uc2  The bank on Wednesday spent $70 million in a total volume of $97.1 million, and went on to plough in $142.4 million more on Thursday - 93 percent of the day's total trade of $149.9 million. The bank conceded it was one of its heaviest \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  ever. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has twice modified the exchange rate band in the last year. In the three days before the de facto peso devaluation last September, it sold about $217 million in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. In the two days before the June 27 devaluation, it sold about $284 million. \par \par \uc2  In contrast with local analysts, Wall Street believed Colombia's trading band would not necessarily be scrapped any time soon. \par \par \uc2  A group of Wall Street analysts consulted by Reuters said they expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue defending the peso until at least the announcement of a final deal with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Still, U.S. and Colombian analysts appeared to concur that the peso could drop as low as 2,200 or even 2,300 if the band is eliminated. \par \par \uc2  But any decline seems likely to be short term, they say, with few suggesting the peso is fundamentally overvalued. Most believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to return to around current levels in the mid-term. \par \par \uc2  Adding to pressure on Colombia's peso is the sense that trading bands have passed their prime in Latin America. Chile allowed its peso to float freely early this month, following similar moves earlier in the year by Brazil and Ecuador. \par \par \uc2  Colombia, like many other emerging markets, saw a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  control regime in 1994 as a way of controlling the rate of depreciation against the dollar and providing exporters with predictable exchange rates. It was also seen as a mechanism to control inflation, which averaged well above 20 percent in 1994. Inflation has now slowed and seems likely to end 1999 at around 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  In contrast with many of his counterparts in Colombia, Juan Jose Echavarria, head of independent economic think-tank Fedesarrollo, argued against any swift end to the peso trading band. \par \par \uc2  "Now is not the time to do it. It would be tantamount to rewarding all those who were betting on the casino economy," he said. "These markets have been like a betting casino with both sides seeing how much money the other has." \par \par \uc2  ((-Bogota newsroom (571) 523 7877, bogota.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dv9n035bc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
