\par\par \b \uc2  Latin America  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Colombia Firmer On Ctrl Bank \b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 1999\par 21:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly softer against the dollar late Friday except for \b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso, which ended firmer owing to an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  To halt a seven-session slide, Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The peso's closing exchange rate fell to 1850.00 to the dollar from 1853.00 to the dollar Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Activity today aimed to make the market volatile in an attempt to keep speculators away," a Boston-based financial economist said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Mexico's peso closed weaker as investors, following the trend of U.S. Treasurys, placed dollar orders ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  In Mexico City on 48-hour spot dollar markets, banking leader Banamex quoted the peso closing at a mid-rate of MXN9.3630 per dollar, weaker from its close Thursday of MXN9.3300 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real continued its soft streak in sluggish trading on a dearth of inflows, traders said. A state holiday in the country's economic and financial center Sao Paulo has also put a cap on activity. \par \par \uc2  Around 1855 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded down 1.8040/dlr vs. 1.8020 at Thursday's closing. \par \par \uc2  In Venezuela, the bolivar closed weaker for a seventh consecutive session amid moderate corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The bolivar closed at a mid-rate of VEB609.75 to the dollar, versus Thursday's close of VEB609 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken further, although it remains comfortably on the strong end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's wide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. Its current band trading system allows the bolivar to weaken about 1.28% each month. \par \par \uc2  Below is a summary of regional exchange rates in late trading, compared to one U.S. dollar: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                              Friday        Thursday(Close)\par Brazilian real (BRR)         1.8040             1.8020\par Mexican peso (MXN)           9.3630 *           9.3300\par Venezuelan bolivar (VEB)   609.7500 *         609.0000\par Peruvian sol (PEN)           3.3194 *           3.3175\par Ecuadorean sucre (ECS)      12,0000            11,9700\par Colombian peso (COP)      1850.0000 *        1853.0000\par Chilean peso (CLP)         516.7000 *         516.7000\par    *Closing Price\par    Note: Argentine peso is fixed 1:1 against the U.S. dollar under a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board regime.\par -By Angela Pruitt; (201) 938-2269;\par  angela.pruitt@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 09-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1941GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1093 1835 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv79058ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
